 "sources": [
  "Connecticut Agricultural Experiment Station, House Centipede (Scutigera coleoptrata) fact sheet, K. Dugas: 15 pairs of legs; antennae look like legs so predators can't tell which way it faces; fast runners that chase prey; forcipules (modified pincer-like legs) give a venomous sting to prey; stinging a human is very rare and only from rough handling; flees when discovered; hunts cockroach nymphs, bed bugs, flies, ants, silverfish, spiders; nocturnal; https://portal.ct.gov/-/media/caes/documents/publications/fact_sheets/entomology/house_centipede_scutigera.pdf",
  "Washington State University Extension PestSense, House centipede: hunts spiders and insects including flies, cockroaches, moths; usually active at night; https://pestsense.cahnrs.wsu.edu/fact-sheet/house-centipede/",
  "Penn State Extension, House Centipedes: 15 pairs of legs; feeds on silverfish, firebrats, carpet beetle larvae, cockroaches, spiders; comes out at night; harmless; https://extension.psu.edu/house-centipedes",
  "Washington State University Extension PestSense, House centipede: newly hatched nymphs have four pairs of legs, with more pairs added at each molt; https://pestsense.cahnrs.wsu.edu/fact-sheet/house-centipede/ (CAES fact sheet above: upwards of 3 years to mature, can live as long as 7 years, frequents basements, cellars and first-floor bathrooms, most are never seen, pesticides not recommended, reduce humidity and exclude the insects they prey on; Penn State above: the female's last pair of legs is nearly twice the body length)"
 ],
 "beats": [
